---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import CompoundInterestCalculator from '@components/islands/CompoundInterestCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'compound-interest-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is compound interest?',
    answer:
      'Compound interest is interest earned on both your original principal and on the interest already added. Because interest earns interest, savings grow faster over time — an effect that accelerates the longer you stay invested.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does compounding frequency affect growth?',
    answer:
      'The more often interest is compounded — daily versus monthly versus annually — the more you earn, because interest is added to the balance sooner and starts earning itself. The difference is small at low rates and grows at higher rates and longer horizons.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does adding regular contributions do?',
    answer:
      'Adding a fixed amount each period dramatically increases the final balance, because each contribution also begins compounding. The calculator shows how much of your ending balance came from contributions versus interest.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the rule of 72?',
    answer:
      'A quick estimate: divide 72 by your annual rate to approximate the years it takes to double your money. At 8%, that is about 9 years. Use the calculator for an exact figure.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I calculate compound interest?',
    answer:
      'The formula is A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt), where P is the principal, r the annual rate as a decimal, n the times it compounds per year, and t the years. $10,000 at 5% compounded monthly for 10 years grows to about $16,470 — the calculator does this for you and adds any contributions.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between simple and compound interest?',
    answer:
      'Simple interest is charged only on the original principal, so it grows in a straight line. Compound interest is charged on the principal plus accumulated interest, so it accelerates. Over long horizons the gap is enormous — see the simple interest calculator for the contrast.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="See how your savings or investments grow with compound interest and regular contributions. Adjust the rate, time and compounding frequency and press Calculate to see the difference compounding makes."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-10' }}
>
  <CompoundInterestCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter your <strong>initial amount</strong> — the lump sum you are starting with (enter 0 if you are starting from nothing).</li>
      <li>Set the expected <strong>annual interest rate</strong> as a percentage.</li>
      <li>Choose the number of <strong>years</strong> the money will stay invested.</li>
      <li>Pick how often interest <strong>compounds</strong> — annually, monthly or daily.</li>
      <li>Optionally add a <strong>regular contribution</strong> you will make each period.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The calculator returns your future balance and splits it into three parts: the
      principal you started with, the contributions you added along the way, and the
      interest those amounts earned. The balance-by-year table shows the growth curve
      steepening as interest compounds on itself.
    </p>

    <h2>What compound interest is</h2>
    <p>
      Compound interest is interest paid on your original money <em>and</em> on the
      interest it has already earned. <strong>Simple interest</strong> pays only on the
      original principal, so a balance grows in a straight line. <strong>Compound
      interest</strong> pays on the growing balance, so each period's interest is slightly
      larger than the last and the curve bends upward.
    </p>
    <p>
      Put $1,000 in an account paying 10% and the difference is stark. Simple interest
      adds a flat $100 every year. Compounded annually, you earn $100 in year one, $110 in
      year two (you now have $1,100 working for you), then $121 — the gap widens every
      year and never stops. See the <a href="/finance/simple-interest-calculator">simple
      interest calculator</a> for the straight-line contrast.
    </p>

    <h2>The compound interest formula</h2>
    <p>
      For a one-time deposit with no further contributions, the future value is:
    </p>
    <p><strong>A = P(1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup></strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A</strong> — the ending balance (the number you are solving for)</li>
      <li><strong>P</strong> — the principal, your starting amount</li>
      <li><strong>r</strong> — the annual interest rate as a decimal (5% = 0.05)</li>
      <li><strong>n</strong> — how many times interest compounds per year</li>
      <li><strong>t</strong> — the number of years</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      When you add a fixed amount every period, each deposit begins compounding from the
      day it lands, so the calculator adds the future value of that stream of
      contributions on top of the balance above. The maths is fiddlier by hand, which is
      exactly what the tool is for.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Say you deposit <strong>$10,000</strong> at a <strong>5%</strong> annual rate,
      compounded <strong>monthly</strong>, for <strong>10 years</strong>, with no extra
      contributions. Here <em>P</em> = 10,000, <em>r</em> = 0.05, <em>n</em> = 12 and
      <em>t</em> = 10, so:
    </p>
    <p><strong>A = 10,000 × (1 + 0.05/12)<sup>12 × 10</sup> ≈ $16,470</strong></p>
    <p>
      You put in $10,000 and finished with about $16,470 — roughly <strong>$6,470</strong>
      of interest, without adding a single dollar. Stretch the same deposit to 30 years
      and it grows to about $44,700: the extra two decades more than triple the interest,
      because the later years compound on a much bigger balance.
    </p>

    <h2>How compounding frequency affects growth</h2>
    <p>
      More frequent compounding adds interest to your balance sooner, so it starts earning
      on itself sooner. The effect is real but modest — and it shrinks with each step up in
      frequency. Using the same $10,000 at 5% for 10 years with no contributions:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Compounding frequency</th><th>Ending balance</th><th>Interest earned</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Annually</td><td>$16,288.95</td><td>$6,288.95</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quarterly</td><td>$16,436.19</td><td>$6,436.19</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Monthly</td><td>$16,470.09</td><td>$6,470.09</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Daily</td><td>$16,486.65</td><td>$6,486.65</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Moving from annual to monthly compounding adds about $181 here; going all the way to
      daily adds only $17 more. The rate and the time horizon move the needle far more than
      the compounding frequency does.
    </p>

    <h2>Why starting early beats saving more later</h2>
    <p>
      Because interest earns interest, the years at the <em>end</em> of a long horizon do
      the heaviest lifting — and you only get those years by starting early. An investor
      who saves modestly through their twenties often ends up ahead of someone who saves
      far more but doesn't begin until their forties. Time in the market, not the size of
      any single deposit, is usually the deciding factor.
    </p>
    <p>
      Regular contributions amplify this, because <strong>every deposit starts its own
      compounding clock</strong>. Add $200 a month to that $10,000 at 5% and you finish the
      10 years with roughly $47,000 — the contributions total $24,000, and compounding
      supplies the rest.
    </p>

    <h2>The rule of 72</h2>
    <p>
      For a quick estimate of how long an amount takes to double, divide 72 by the annual
      rate. At 8% that is about <strong>9 years</strong>; at 6%, about <strong>12
      years</strong>. It is an approximation — use the calculator for an exact figure — but
      it is a handy way to sanity-check whether a given rate is doing much for you.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations to keep in mind</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Constant-rate assumption.</strong> The tool applies one steady rate. Real savings and investment returns fluctuate and can be negative in any given year.</li>
      <li><strong>Before inflation.</strong> Results are in today's nominal dollars; inflation erodes real buying power over time. Translate a projection with the <a href="/finance/inflation-calculator">inflation calculator</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Before tax and fees.</strong> Interest in a taxable account is usually taxed, and investment fees reduce returns — neither is deducted here.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To model a portfolio with monthly contributions, use the
      <a href="/finance/investment-calculator">investment calculator</a>; to project
      compounding all the way to retirement, try the
      <a href="/finance/retirement-calculator">retirement calculator</a>. This is
      educational information, not financial advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
